</svg>inksheaf</b><span>private beta · printed in the US</span></header>

  <main>
    <div class="herowind" aria-hidden="true">
      <video src="/hero-pages.mp4" poster="/hero-pages-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline
        preload="metadata" width="1280" height="518"></video>
    </div>
    <h1 class="reveal">See the book inside your archive.</h1>
    <p class="lede reveal">Paste your Substack URL. Inksheaf turns the last year of public writing
    into a 6×9 edition in your publication's masthead and palette.</p>

    <div class="hero-workbench reveal">
    <div class="tryit reveal" id="tryit">
      <label for="tryurl">See your own book. Paste your publication's URL:</label>
      <div class="tryrow">
        <input type="url" id="tryurl" placeholder="https://yourname.substack.com" autocomplete="off" />
        <button id="trybtn" type="button">Show my book</button>
      </div>
      <div class="tryhint note">Reads only your public archive. Preview numbers are cached for 24 hours; post bodies are not read or stored.</div>
      <div class="tryerr" id="tryerr" role="alert"></div>
      <button class="tryhandoff" id="tryhandoff" type="button" hidden>Send me a hand-built preview</button>
    </div>

    <section class="preview on" id="preview" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Book preview">
      <div class="pv-stage" id="pv-stage" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="setkick">Setting your edition</div>
        <div class="setline" id="pv-setline">Reading your archive&hellip;</div>
        <div class="setrule"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pv-grid">
        <div class="bookobj" id="pvbook" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="cover">
            <div class="cv-kind" id="pv-kind">Annual edition</div>
            <div class="cv-mast" id="pv-mast">Your publication</div>
            <div class="cv-rule"></div>
            <div class="cv-dates" id="pv-dates">The past twelve months</div>
            <div class="cv-mid"></div>
            <div class="cv-foot"><span class="cv-pages" id="pv-cvpages">6 × 9 · perfect bound</span><span class="cv-vol">Inksheaf</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="spinefold"></div>
          <div class="spinetext"><span id="pv-spine">Your publication</span></div>
          <div class="pageblock" id="pv-block"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pv-page" id="pv-page" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="run"><span id="pv-runname">Your publication</span><span>Contents</span></div>
          <div id="pv-toc">
            <div class="toc-line"><span class="t">Your first piece</span><span class="dots"></span><span class="n">9</span></div>
            <div class="toc-line"><span class="t">Another from the archive</span><span class="dots"></span><span class="n">17</span></div>
            <div class="toc-line"><span class="t">The writing readers keep</span><span class="dots"></span><span class="n">31</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="fol">&middot; iv &middot;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="pv-facts">
          <div class="pv-big" id="pv-big"></div>
          <p class="pv-sub" id="pv-sub"></p>
          <div class="pv-price" id="pv-price"></div>
          <button class="pv-cta" id="pv-cta" type="button">Reserve a beta spot for this book</button>
          <p class="pv-note note">Page count is an estimate from your public archive; the proof PDF is exact.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="kicker">The object</h2>
      <p>One publication, typeset. These are actual pages from the production file of
      the <a href="https://www.caithrin.com">caithrin.com</a> edition: 40 essays, 294 pages at 6×9,
      the same file the printed copies come from.</p>
      <div class="realspread">
        <img src="/spread-toc-real.jpg" alt="Actual contents spread from the caithrin edition"
          loading="lazy" width="1800" height="1359" />
        <img src="/spread-real.jpg" alt="Actual essay spread with running headers and article notes"
          loading="lazy" width="1800" height="1359" />
      </div>
      <p class="note">Not a mockup: these spreads are rendered from the production PDF. Photographs
      of the printed copy replace them the day it arrives.</p>
      <table class="spec">
        <tr><td>Trim</td><td>6 × 9 in, perfect bound</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paper</td><td>60# uncoated white, black ink</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cover</td><td>Full colour, matte, your publication's own masthead and palette</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Length</td><td>32–300 pages per volume; longer archives become a quarterly set</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Apparatus</td><td>Contents with page numbers, running headers, article notes and bylines per article</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>Beta pricing is at cost: readers pay exactly what printing and shipping
        cost, and we add nothing. Measured on a real order: a 294-page collected edition landed for
        $16.58 including tax, mainland US</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Live example</td><td>The first edition is on sale now, at its exact $9.34 print cost:
        <a href="https://www.lulu.com/shop/caithrin-rintoul/caithrin-collected-essays-20252026/paperback/product-m2v2e82.html">caithrin
        &mdash; Collected Essays 2025&ndash;2026</a>. You pay the printer directly; neither we nor the
        author receive a cent on it.</td></tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="kicker">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="how">
        <li>Paste your publication's URL above. The instant specimen uses public archive metadata;
        paid writing enters the concierge beta through Substack's own export, never your password.</li>
        <li>We typeset a print-ready edition and send you the exact proof PDF. You mark corrections;
        we make them and ask for written approval before printing.</li>
        <li>Once approved, the edition is published through Lulu. Lulu prints each copy on demand,
        takes the reader's payment and ships it directly.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="kicker">Who the beta is for</h2>
      <p>Substack writers with roughly 1,000 to 20,000 subscribers, at least a year of archive, and
      essays or serialized nonfiction. Every publication in the beta gets concierge treatment: a
      person, not a pipeline, checks every page of your first issue.</p>
      <p>The clearest beta uses are an annual readers buy from an About page, a quarterly set for
      frequent publishers, or a physical door into the back catalog for new subscribers.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 class="kicker">Questions writers ask</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>What does it cost me?</dt>
        <dd>Nothing during the beta. Readers pay printing and shipping at cost, with nothing added:
        a 294-page book landed at $16.58 on a real order, and thinner books cost less.</dd>
        <dt>What do I earn?</dt>
        <dd>During the beta, nothing and neither do we: books sell at cost. The author revenue share
        gets designed with beta publications afterward, on measured numbers.</dd>
        <dt>Who owns the writing?</dt>
        <dd>You do. Nothing prints until you approve the proof. Beta permissions and takedown terms
        are confirmed in writing before an edition is listed.</dd>
        <dt>How do paid posts get in without my password?</dt>
        <dd>You upload the export Substack generates in your own dashboard (Settings → Exports).
        Any subscriber files inside it are discarded unread; only the posts are kept.</dd>
        <dt>What happens to images, tweets and embeds?</dt>
        <dd>The renderer reports missing images and replaces web-only embeds with printed source
        notes. During beta, we inspect that report and the proof with you before printing.</dd>
        <dt>When do the first books exist?</dt>
        <dd>The beta targets December printing, with books in readers' hands around New Year. Beta
        publications hear every date change directly.</dd>
        <dt>Where can I read more?</dt>
        <dd>Write to <a href="mailto:hello@inksheaf.com">hello@inksheaf.com</a>; every message gets
        an answer. The build is documented as it happens on
        <a href="https://www.caithrin.com">caithrin.com</a>.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="beta" id="beta">
      <h2 class="kicker">Join the private beta</h2>
      <div class="steps">Step <b id="stepnum">1</b> of 2</div>
      <form id="f" novalidate>
        <input class="hp" type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />

        <div class="step1">
